In an attempt to climb out of the hole it jumped into last Monday, Compaq
has apparently been energetically leaking information (from employees
purporting to speak on their behalf) about Intel's having internally
perceived the error of its ways and now wishing to fix the problems in the
IA64 architecture by enlisting much or all of the Alpha architecture in the
service of the IA64 instruction set.  Particularly explicit quotes include:

From a Compaq employee in comp.os.linux.alpha (6/26 4:48 A.M. EDT and 6/28
4:03 A.M. EDT):

"Alpha is not dead, its technologie will be implemented in the Itanium v3
processor of intel which will be released end of 2003, early 2004."

"All what was planned to be in the EV8 chip will be implemented in the intel
itanium v3.  The engineers who are developing the alpha are transfered to
intel."

And in comp.os.vms from a non-employee reporting what he was told by Compaq
engineers (and Mark Gorham, who may be the new head VMS honcho - I've lost
track):

6/28 9:28 P.M. EDT:

"With Alpha engineers working on Itanium-II (read: fixing what's wrong with
Itanium-I with Alpha technology) ... Compaq is helping Intel design the next
generation of IA64 which will be based on Alpha technology and optimized for
Compaq operating systems."

6/29 9:11 A.M. EDT:

"Simply put, Intel has finally admitted, at least privately, they don't know
how to design a 64 bit processor.  They need decent engineers, and that's
where the xfer of Alpha engineers to Intel fits ... That's the EV8 team's
task.  Build a better Itanium than anything Intel or HP could, and call it
Itanium-II."

See also 6/28 8:28 P.M. EDT for more near-quotes-from-Compaq-types leading
up to the above.  There were other earlier similar (but less explicit)
allegations by others, but I didn't have the patience to go back and track
them down.  The Inquirer also took note of such activity in an article last
Thursday:

http://www.theinquirer.net/28060117.htm

"Today's spin from Compaq employees has ranged from assertions that the
Alpha line was incapable of further performance increases, to the
reassurance that the next generation of IA64 chips will be re-branded
Alphas."

Leaving aside the fact that none of this will necessarily help avoid
increased doubts about Compaq's commitment to anything non-Wintel and
short-and-medium-term negative impact of the coming platform-switch on
Alpha, VMS, and Tru64 sales, there's still the interesting question of how
Intel and HP feel about such allegations about the inadequacy of their shiny
new IA64 architecture.  Even if the allegations are true, reports that Intel
is now going to start over from an Alpha base won't exactly be good news for
Itanium/McKinley acceptance over the next couple of years, so what's good
for Compaq's ability to rationalize its divestiture of Alpha may be bad for
Intel/HP business, and vice versa.  And if the allegations are false, then
one might expect Intel and HP to be more than a little miffed.

I took a quick look today at comp.sys.hp.hardware and comp.sys.hp.hpux - not
a peep there about anything Alpha-related.  Comp.sys.intel discussion seemed
to center on how Compaq needed Alpha to maintain credibility in their
high-end business - i.e., surprisingly positive sentiments about Alpha (from
a group with their name), even before the announcement was official and they
knew they could afford to be generous.

So I clearly haven't done any exhaustive research (and have no idea where
such discussion might hang out), but so far haven't stumbled upon a peep
from Intel, HP, or anyone purporting to speak on their behalf on the matter
of Compaq's (alleged) allegations of IA64 inadequacies and its rescue by
Alpha technology.  If anyone else has, could they provide a pointer?

Thanks,

- bill

[I don't usually cross-post this widely, but I did take care to include only
groups where active discussion of Alpha's demise both seemed appropriate and
had already occurred.]





