Fernando Nogueira wrote:
> 
> Hi.
> 
> I am having troubles running some of my fortran codes on Tru64 Unix 5.0 on a
> XP1000 workstation (and on a PW500au too). The message I get is that the
> datasize is too low. However I cannot increase it beyond 1GByte. I used to
> do it in Digital Unix 4.0d, before upgrading to the newer OS. Someone
> suggested me I should try linux, and that solved this 1 GByte limitation.
> But now I have a stacksize problem - it is only 8 kBytes and only root can
> increase it... and I don't want to run my codes as root :-)).
> 
> Did anyone have these problems? How can I solve them?
> 
> Thanks,
> 
> Fernando

The 1Gbyte limit for max datasize is not a limitation of the OS, just
the way the O/S has been configured. Tru64 has settings for default and
max sizes for process limits. There are values built in at kernel build
time but these can be changed by values read from /etc/sysconfigtab at
boot time. The simplest way to adjust them on a workstation is use
dxkerneltuner. Run this under root and select the proc subsystem which
display the current values and give you a box where you can set the boot
values you want. The ones you are interested in are:

max-per-proc-address-space  
per-proc-address-space 
max-per-proc-data-size 
per-proc-data-size 
max-per-proc-stack-size 
per-proc-stack-size 

Which set the max and default values for a process. You probably need to
change vm-maxvas in the vm subsystem as well to increase the max virtual
size. 

Having done this then following a reboot you should get the new higher
values you entered.

Nick Hill
ITD SCS Group


