I'll believe it when I see it...  I seriously doubt VMS will make its way to
Itanium, regardless of what Compaq says.  They will fall upon tough economic
times (like now) and will simply let it die just like they have let VAX and
now ALPHA die a slow painful death.  I still recall Oracle claiming that RDB
would be released for NT.  If it has, nobody is using it....

As for True64 on Itanium, it could happen, but why when Compaq sells a lot
more Linux servers than they do True64.  What I could envision is Compaq
taking the Linux kernel, adding lots of nice True64 features to it, and
providing their own distribution for Itanium.    Also, do you really think
ISV's are chomping at the bit to develop for VMS and True64 on Itanium?  Sun
has won the Unix battle.  Solaris prevails in the industry.  VMS is dying,
despite its superior design.  Alpha is dying, despite its superior design.
Microsoft is still winning on most fronts, and will continue to improve
their products, thereby making further inroads into the data-centers of the
enterprise.

At work, we use lots of VMS systems and just started using True64.  Both are
nice, however neither is mainstream any longer.  ISV's always support these
OS's as second or third tier these days.  Too bad Digital couldn't have seen
the way to properly market their awesome technology.  Intel, Oracle,
Microsoft, Sun, and others figured it out and have won.  It is time to move
on...  (Note:  I still like VMS and ALPHA, I'm just a pragmatist.;)

