In article <9gmunj$bok$1@venus.telepac.pt>, "Fernando Nogueira" <fnog@teor.fis.uc.pt> writes:

>I am having troubles running some of my fortran codes on Tru64 Unix 5.0 on a
>XP1000 workstation (and on a PW500au too). The message I get is that the
>datasize is too low. However I cannot increase it beyond 1GByte. I used to
>do it in Digital Unix 4.0d, before upgrading to the newer OS. Someone
>suggested me I should try linux, and that solved this 1 GByte limitation.
>But now I have a stacksize problem - it is only 8 kBytes and only root can
>increase it... and I don't want to run my codes as root :-)).

The maximum and default stack and data size limits are system
configuration parameters.  Read the manual page for the sysconfig
command to see the various ways of changing these values.  The
limits come in two values, the system maximum and the default.
Many shells allow raising these limits from the default to the
maximum.  A good shell will have the command it uses to change
these limits documented somewhere.

On my V4.0D system, I can raise the stack size to 32 MB using the
command:

% limit stack unlimited

I happen to be using csh(1).  If you can't raise the limit for the
stack on your system, first see what the default and maximum are.
If your system manager set them to the same value, then it makes
sense that it can't be raise.  If you're already at the maximum
and need more, go back to my text on sysconfig(8).

