Christian Weisgerber <naddy@mips.inka.de> wrote:

> There is no SRM for the Samsung PC164UX board.  You're stuck with
> ARCSBIOS.  Thus no Tru64 or BSD.

Yeap!

> You can run Linux on it, although
> the installation is somewhat painful and I'm not sure whether any
> current distribution still supports it.

I do not know about "painful".  If you have both CD and floppy drives
then you need a distribution CD and one floppy with a ramdisk made from
an image on said CD. That is about it.  If you mean by "pain" that
anaconda will not load ramdisk transparently from CD, but you will have
to ask for it from a floppy, then so be it.  An initial milo, while
installing for the first time, can be loaded from CD.  Leave a small FAT
partition (some 3-4 Megs) on your boot disk to store milo, its loader
and maybe a "rescue boot image" (i.e. a kernel good enough to boot your
basic system without using any modules) on it.  It is a good idea to
make this partion with a help of tools included in ARCSBIOS, and create
other partitions with Linux tools, although you may do everything from
Linux.  Menus can be later set to autoboot Linux on a power-up even if
ARCSBIOS makes this into a head-scratcher. :-)

ARCSBIOS needs its "private" first stage loader ('ldmilo' and not
'linload' used with other ARC machines, IIRC).  Oh, versions of milos
for UX included on 6.x CDs from Red Hat were indeed broken and I cannot
really tell you about the status right now.  This is forcing booting
from two floppies, if you do not have a working milo on your hard disk
yet, after replacing a broken milo with another version from the net.
The first "milo" floppy is in a FAT format so this is not a brain
surgery either.

Red Hat 7.0 definitely installs on UX without any fuss and most likely
an incoming 7.1 as well (although I do not have now a machine to test
that).  Do not allow your startup scripts to use '-A' flag to hwclock,
even if some versions pretend to be smarter than you are, or you will
have your system clock off by twenty years.

I expect anything you can practically find to be installable on UX.
Yes, doing that from SRM is indeed tad simpler. :-)  There are likely
some detailed UX installation write-ups on www.alphalinux.org.

  Michal


