erevos@mailbox.gr (Erevos) wrote in message news:<b9cd0ee3.0106130215.3cb313c3@posting.google.com>...
> Hello,
>
> I am working as a customer service engineer, for some years on Intel
> based linux machines and AlphaServers with Digital Unix. I would like
> to try installing linux on an AlphaServer 1000, using only the source
> code.

Hi,

I would like to post my experiences here trying to do just that. I got
very interested in installing Linux From Scratch (LFS) on my Alpha
UP1000/SuSE 6.4 and so downloaded all the source code recommended by LFS
and also the LFS installation manual. I converted this to an installation
script after a lot of time and effort since typing in commands over and
over would have amounted to a lot more, especially if I had to re-install.

When I made my first attempt to compile LFS sources, I saw a lot of error
messages from egcs and some sources failed to compile. I started again,
diverting stderror to a file and let the script complete. Hey presto, I
ended up with a big file full of warnings, error messages and compilation
failure messages from egcs. After a lot of one-at-a-time source code
compilation attempts, I realized that it just was not possible to compile
a fair proportion of packages. In particular, I never managed to compile
egcs itself so I could not even attempt to chroot into the LFS partition
and recompile the LFS sources.

After consultation with the LFS forums and some searching in linux forums
etc, I realized that I was asking for too much, to successfully compile
standard sources on my Alpha. This included many sources of basic GNU/
linux packages as well as others that are needed in a minimal working
installation. Soon I realized the hard way that even in linux, we are
living in a PC-centric world, were a fair proportion of sources are
written for 32-bit CISC type processors, with no consideration for
'outsiders'. Most of the warnings and errors from egcs related to 64-bit
incompatibility (casting of pointer to integer of different size and
similar) and running successful compilation usually produced runtime
messages like unaligned traps etc.

Determined to continue, I then decided to use sources from my SuSE
distribution but still had compilation failures for some reason, so
I decided to install some compiled packages but then rpm refused to
install in the LFS partition due to dependencies etc. I also had the
same problem downloading either sources or binaries from the net,
mostly again due to package managers refusing to do their thing.
Eventually I tried some sources and binaries from Slackware where
I had much better results, because the tar.gz based distribution
was less fussy working outside the distribution's installation and
the sorces seemed to compile easier (even managed to get egcs to
compile!).

I did eventually manage to install a clonky LFS on the UP1000, but it
was not really usable so I am now waiting for a stable Slackware
release for the Alpha for my hacking activities, using SuSE 7.0 to
complete some software projects.

My conclusion was, till PC's go 64-bit, whenever that may be, using
standard sources to install Alpha linux seems out of the question.
And I wonder what will happen when/if 64-bit PC's come out, a lot
of sourcecode re-writing will surely be needed!

Please excuse my long posting, it came out of the heart rather than
the head!


