Just like the situation I had.  Otherwise things went well,
filesystems got synced correctly and init stopped itself but when
kernel was supposed to quit and fall back to aboot or SRM...  That
always failed.

> Using SRM+MILO works? Cool.

It definitely does, at least on some Alpha systems.  MILO never
crashes like SRM or aboot did, since it never quits.  Once MILO starts
up, there is no way back to SRM (AFAIK).  Except for power switch,
that is.

I am running MILO from floppy disk instead of writing it on FLASH ROM
since it is the most convenient way for me to handle booting.

> Well, MILO provides it's own PALcode which Linux then uses instead
> of the one from SRM, so it is likely that the SRM version you have
> flashed is just buggy or incompatible with Linux.

I think it is still the latest one available.  I never remember what
version it is but it has not been updated for several years.

> Also Debian GNU/Linux here, the latest Potato version (installed via
