Patrick Walker writes:

> Nope.  Arc command doesn't work.

AXPpci can only have SRM or ARC, not both at the same time.  You are
supposed to choose between two when you run the firmware flashing
utility.

My AXPpci 33 came with ARC when I bought it, which I later changed
into SRM.  At this point I thought something is broken with the
kernel, as Linux was still running fine but kept crashing easily if
load got above 1.00.  It also never got through shutdown without
crashing when kernel quit, but each time I wanted to reboot, power
switch was needed.  For example, I had to boot few times while
compiling (Linux) kernel.  This I got solved by booting from SRM with
MILO instead of aboot.  After that, system was as stable as I expected
it to be.

It still boots with MILO, but now loads it directly through "Fail-safe
Loader Floppy" setting of SROM Select jumpers.  Biggest problem with
MILO is that it only supports wintel-style partition tables, so kernel
needs to be on a disk with one.  Maybe that is not even the case with
newest MILO versions since recent kernels support quite a few types
partitition tables.

I am still running Debian GNU/Linux.  I wonder if anyone else has had
this kind of problem with AXPpci 33 board.  Did you find alternative
solutions?

